cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "DLOG-QML: Quantum learning on a cryptographically hidden coordinate system"
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below.
  Please ALSO cite Liu, Arunachalam & Temme (2021), which is the source of the
  concept class, the coset-state feature map, and the asymptotic separation
  theorem. This repository is an independent reproducible implementation and
  falsification study; it does not claim the underlying theory as novel.
type: software
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-28"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A reproducible, falsifiable finite-size study of the discrete-logarithm
  concept class of Liu, Arunachalam and Temme (Nature Physics 17, 1013, 2021).
  A binary concept whose label depends on its input only through a discrete
  logarithm is presented to learners that observe only the binary encoding of
  the corresponding group element. Over 20 seeds at p = 1019, a Shor-style
  coset-state quantum kernel reaches 98.9 +/- 1.8 per cent held-out accuracy
  while eleven competing models -- including a tuned RBF-SVM, gradient
  boosting, an MLP and a strong generic hardware-efficient quantum kernel --
  cluster between 49.0 and 54.1 per cent, none significantly above chance.
  The work ships four falsification controls (a generic quantum kernel, a
  polynomial-time Legendre/Euler attack that breaks a naive variant of the
  construction, a hidden-coordinate leakage oracle, and a mismatched-concept
  test on which the quantum kernel itself fails), paired significance testing,
  and explicit separation of measured results from theoretical and asymptotic
  claims. The instance is deliberately small and classically tractable; no
  cryptographic-scale advantage and no quantum hardware result is claimed.
keywords:
  - quantum machine learning
  - quantum kernel methods
  - discrete logarithm
  - concept class
  - reproducibility
  - falsification
  - qiskit
authors:
  - name: "DLOG-QML contributors"
repository-code: "https://github.com/REPLACE-ME/dlog-qml"
